<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georges de La Tour</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1593">1593</a>–<a href="/years/1652">1652</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georges de La Tour was a painter from the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> who worked mainly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a>. He is known for religious and genre scenes, many lit by a single candle, influenced by the followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>, and he received the title of painter in ordinary to Louis XIII. Forgotten after his death, his work was rediscovered in the early twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georges-de-la-tour.jpg" alt="Georges de La Tour" width="320" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges de La Tour · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Tour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Georges de La Tour was born on 13 March 1593 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vic-sur-seille">Vic-sur-Seille</a>, in the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, a town technically part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> but ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> since 1552. Baptism records show that he was the son of Jean de La Tour, a baker, and Sybille de La Tour, née Molian, and the second of seven children. His education is unclear. It is assumed that he travelled early in his career to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and he may have trained under Jacques Bellange in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a>.</p><p>His paintings reflect the naturalism of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>, which probably reached him through the Dutch Caravaggisti of the Utrecht School, and he is often compared to Hendrick Terbrugghen. In 1617 he married Diane Le Nerf, from a minor noble family, and in 1620 he set up a studio in her home town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a> in the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>La Tour worked for the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> in 1623–4 and received the title Painter to the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1638, but the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> were his main market and he achieved a certain affluence. He is not recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a> between 1639 and 1642 and may have travelled again, and Anthony Blunt saw the influence of Gerrit van Honthorst in his work afterwards. He took part in a Franciscan-led religious revival in Lorraine and came to paint almost entirely religious subjects, treated with the influence of genre painting.</p><p>His early work includes genre scenes of cheats, as in The Fortune Teller, and fighting beggars, derived from the Dutch Caravaggisti and probably from Bellange. He often painted several versions of one subject, and his surviving output is relatively small.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>La Tour is best known for nocturnal light effects, developed further than his predecessors had done, and carried from the genre subjects of the Dutch Caravaggisti into religious painting. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>, his religious paintings lack dramatic effects. In a second phase of his style, perhaps from the 1640s, he used chiaroscuro, careful geometrical compositions and very simplified forms, and his work moved towards greater simplicity and stillness.</p><p>His son Étienne, born in 1621, was his pupil, and it is difficult to tell their hands apart in versions of La Tour's compositions, as with the Education of the Virgin in the Frick Collection. La Tour and his family died in an epidemic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a> in 1652. He was forgotten after his death, and some of his work was confused with that of Vermeer. The German art historian Hermann Voss rediscovered him in 1915, and he later inspired writers such as René Char, André Malraux, Pascal Quignard and Charles Juliet.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Georges de La Tour” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The printmaker Jacques Callot of Nancy recorded the horrors of the Thirty Years’ War in The Miseries and Misfortunes of War (1633), <b>Georges de La Tour</b> worked at Lunéville, and the landscape painter Claude Lorrain, born at Chamagne, took his name from his homeland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vic-sur-seille">Vic-sur-Seille</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.78,6.53&amp;year=1631" aria-label="Vic-sur-Seille on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Georges de La Tour"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5367 5935h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5367 5935h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.78,6.53&amp;year=1631">What was here in 1631? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
